{"id":"chatcmpl-0bdf7f9d-5b3c-4d90-a770-5233f11a84bd","object":"chat.completion","created":1795229586,"model":"deepseek-v4.1-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","reasoning_content":"","content":"```json\n{\n  \"highlights\": \"When a question asks about a finding's location but the provided image is a lateral view, spatial laterality (right vs. left) cannot be read directly and must be inferred from projection geometry. The successful approach was to first verify whether the finding exists at all, then map anterior/posterior position to the correct side using the known view orientation. This avoids being misled by the apparent absence of the finding or by distractor options.\",\n  \"patterns\": [\n    \"When laterality is asked but only a lateral/oblique projection is given, infer right/left from the known projection geometry (which side is against the detector) rather than expecting direct labeling.\",\n    \"Before answering a 'which finding' question, first confirm the finding is actually present; absence of an obvious finding may mean it is subtle, not that the question is invalid.\",\n    \"Use the image's own orientation label (e.g., view/side markers) as the primary key for spatial reasoning, and treat it as authoritative over assumptions.\",\n    \"When a finding's position is ambiguous on a single projection, translate anatomical position (anterior/posterior, upper/lower) into laterality using the projection's known orientation.\"\n  ],\n  \"playbooks\": {\n    \"Medical imaging interpretation\": \"Read orientation labels first; use projection geometry to convert apparent position into anatomical laterality; verify presence of the finding before committing to a location option.\",\n    \"Visual question answering with spatial options\": \"Distinguish 'is it there?' from 'where is it?'; if options differ only by side, resolve side via projection/orientation metadata rather than visual guesswork.\",\n    \"Ambiguous-evidence reasoning\": \"When direct evidence is weak, reason from structural constraints (view type, anatomy) to eliminate impossible options and select the most geometrically consistent one.\"\n  },\n  \"checklists\": [\n    \"Confirm the image orientation/view label and use it to define which anatomical side maps to which image region.\",\n    \"Verify the finding exists and localize it anatomically (anterior/posterior, upper/lower) before converting to right/left laterality.\"\n  ]\n}\n```"},"finish_reason":"stop","logprobs":null}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1017,"total_tokens":1464,"completion_tokens":447,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"image_tokens":0,"text_tokens":1017},"completion_tokens_details":{"text_tokens":447}}}